# 63-vu1-hot-entry: measurement only. GOW_VU1_HOT=1 attributes the VU1 wall time to the entry PC of every program run;
# off by default and, while off, the cost is one static branch per run (no rdtsc).
#
# Patch 62 sampled one block call out of eight and scaled the sum back, which printed 112-124 % of run_ms and could not
# say which microprogram the time belongs to. A run is only ~24k/s, so timing all of them is cheaper and exact: every
# run takes two rdtsc (well under 0.1 % of a core) and adds its wall time, blocks and pairs to hot[entry pc >> 3]. The
# 5 s line prints the total and the eight largest entries (pc, ms, share, runs/s, pairs, ns per pair). It also replaces
# nothing in patch 62, so both can run together; [vu1-hot] total_ms is the part of run_ms that is inside the runs.
#
# Measured (i3-4170 + HD 4400, Asia disc, 0.5x, VU1 x64 fast, GOW_VU1_FAST=1, attract/replay demo, 5 s windows):
#   three entry PCs, and nothing else, own the whole VU1 wall: pc=0 44.1-44.8 % (13.8-16.6 ns/pair), pc=18 29.8-31.0
#   % (13.0-14.8), pc=30 24.7-26.4 % (11.7-13.0), 85M-135M pairs each, 27k-28k runs/s in total. So the wall is the
#   microcode of three VU1 programs (~2350 pairs per run) and not the dispatch layer; patch 62's block share is
#   consistent with that (dispatch 0.0-4.6 % on this build).
